From 8c67e0bd51b37fbcc71dfaf8a73f9585aaca51a3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: wunhuang <wunhuang@amd.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 13:43:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [AMD] DSV4.1: emit MXFP8 operands from their producers on
 gfx950

On the gfx950 MXFP8 dense routes (aiter group32 via --fp8-gemm-backend aiter,
or native), three producers handed their consumer bf16, and the consumer then
ran its own per-32 MXFP8 quant as a separate ~4.5 us launch in every layer:

- shared expert SwiGLU -> down_proj: the half width (2304/TP2 = 1152) is a
  multiple of 128, so aiter's fused_clamp_act_mul ran, which only quantizes for
  a 128x128-block down_proj. Route an MXFP8 down_proj to the Triton
  silu_and_mul_clamp epilogue, which emits fp8 + ue8m0 directly; its
  inter <= 1024 cap was unnecessary (1024-wide programs keep 32-groups whole).
- wo_a -> wo_b: wo_b_takes_fp8_grid only accepted the native backend. Add an
  EMIT_FP8 epilogue to the gfx950 batched wo_a GEMM (single-chain kernel and
  split-K reduce) and hand wo_b an Mxfp8Activation when its route consumes it.
- FFN norm -> shared expert gate_up: the mHC rmsnorm_with_sinkhorn launch
  already supports emit_fp8 next to its bf16 norm; use it for the FFN norm and
  pass the operand to the shared expert through the existing pre_quant_input
  path (the bf16 norm still feeds the router and routed experts).

Each is gated by an env flag, default on under ROCm and off elsewhere:
SGLANG_HIP_SHARED_ACT_MXFP8, SGLANG_HIP_WO_A_MXFP8, SGLANG_HIP_FFN_NORM_MXFP8.

All three are bit-exact against the unfused route (fp8 codes and ue8m0 scales,
decode/verify token counts, split-K on and off). DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash TP2 on
MI355X, DSpark, c1 decode: 1376 -> 1256 kernels per step, GPU time per step
9.458 -> 8.879 ms (-6.1%). GSM8K 5-shot (1319, real draft acceptance): 0.904.
